[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Event Handling & Modifiers in Svelte"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Svelte makes handling DOM events straightforward using the on: directive. Furthermore, it provides built-in event modifiers that allow you to modify event behavior directly in your markup without writing boilerplate code in your event handler functions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Event Handling"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To listen to any DOM event, use the on: directive followed by the event name. You can pass an inline function or reference a handler defined in your script block."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  let count = 0;
  function handleClick(event) {
    count += 1;
  }
</script>

<button on:click={handleClick}>
  Clicks: {count}
</button>"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Event Modifiers"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Svelte allows you to suffix event directives with modifiers using the pipe symbol (|). These modifiers alter the behavior of the underlying DOM event listener."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"preventDefault: Calls event.preventDefault() before running the handler."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"stopPropagation: Calls event.stopPropagation(), preventing the event from bubbling up."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"passive: Improves scrolling performance on touch/wheel events."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"once: Ensures the handler is run at most once."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Chaining Modifiers"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can chain multiple modifiers together by separating them with pipes. For example, to prevent default behavior and ensure the click handler only runs once, use the following syntax:"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"<form on:submit|preventDefault|once={handleSubmit}>
  <button type="submit">Submit Once</button>
</form>"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Component Events"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike DOM elements, components can also dispatch custom events using the createEventDispatcher utility. Consuming components can listen to these custom events using the exact same on: syntax."}]
